Three years after my grandmother's death, I can finally laugh about those early, zombie-like stages of my personal journey with grief. Quick case in point. Once, I bumped into a neighbor on a wooded byway off Gallows Road, a path I chose just to avoid people and howl peacefully after a grocery run. I can only imagine what he thought. It was a tragic, now hilarious mess.

On a serious note, that time in general left such a deep mark that I knew I had to do something with it. After two years of circling the idea, I finally found the nerve to make it real. Sure, time and timing played their parts, but the truth is, fear was what kept me stuck. Who would care about such a heavy topic? You know, the usual, self-sabotaging mind games.

![](/images/uploads/stories/yes-another-podcast-a-grief-podcast/granules%20of%20GRIEF2.png)

But here we are. A post about my new podcast,[ **Granules of Grief with Refiloe**](https://youtu.be/2j9NAzct8vI?si=09SZC4DK-3EmD1PS). As I prepared to share this, a question kept nagging at me: Yes, I've walked [this road](https://youtu.be/2j9NAzct8vI?si=49-OsfzAzaOfI4zy) (not by choice), but what do I really know? Clearer inner voice: Knowing everything about grief might actually ruin the point, silly! Grief isn't something you master. It's something you live, over and over, in all its wild, unpredictable forms. I'm no grief specialist, but my experiences with loss have shaped who I am, and they've left me hungry to explore what it actually means to live with this kind of wound.

Grieving a lost loved one is something so particular, so consuming — which was my answer to the other nagging question of who would care. In the throes of despair, I for one found myself frantically searching for and finding comfort in others' experiences of grief. I hope Granules of Grief with Refiloe attracts those who need some parts or all of it. That we learn. Teach each other, and anyone else open to it. Just talk. Laugh at ourselves (many grievers have concurred with me that grief has elements of humor). My hope for Granules of Grief is that, whatever its shelf life, it becomes a space for us to do what we want with it in ways other spaces do not allow us. I recently recorded the first episode with a soon-to-be-revealed guest, and there were so many good things that I don't want to spoil it with words. I can only thank you for being here and promise to be back with an update soon.
